}
</code></pre> <pre><code>package main

func main() {
    ch := make(chan int, 1)
    for i := 0; i &lt; 10; i++ {
        select {
        case ch &lt;- i:
        case val := &lt;-ch:
            println(val)
        }
    }
}

// Output: 0,2,4,6,8
</code></pre> <p>The output of the program is <code>0, 2, 4, 6, 8</code> because of the way the <code>select</code> statement works with the buffered channel.</p> <p>Here is a step-by-step explanation:</p> <ol> <li>The channel <code>ch</code> is created with a buffer size of 1.</li> <li>The <code>for</code> loop runs 10 times, with <code>i</code> ranging from 0 to 9.</li> <li>In each iteration of the loop, the <code>select</code> statement has two cases:<ul> <li><code>case ch &lt;- i:</code> attempts to send the value of <code>i</code> to the channel.</li> <li><code>case val := &lt;-ch:</code> attempts to receive a value from the channel and assign it to <code>val</code>.</li> </ul> </li> </ol> <p>Since the channel has a buffer size of 1, it can hold only one value at a time. The <code>select</code> statement will choose one of the cases that is ready to proceed.</p> <ul> <li>When <code>i</code> is even (0, 2, 4, 6, 8), the channel is empty, so the <code>ch &lt;- i</code> case is selected, and the value of <code>i</code> is sent to the channel.</li> <li>When <code>i</code> is odd (1, 3, 5, 7, 9), the channel already contains the previous even value, so the <code>val := &lt;-ch</code> case is selected, and the value is received from the channel and printed.</li> </ul> <p>Thus, the program prints the values received from the channel, which are the even numbers <code>0, 2, 4, 6, 8</code>.</p> Необходимо вывести Hello УсловиеРешениеОбъяснение <pre><code>package main
import "fmt"


func main() {
